    <h1>The Listener Inside the Accent Score</h1>
    <p class="essay-deck">Accentedness, comprehensibility and intelligibility as findings about a speaker, a listener and a task</p>
    <p class="essay-byline">Hanpu Li</p>
    <p class="essay-note essay-provenance" role="note">Developed from a 2022 learner-profile assignment. No learner recording, transcription or biographical detail is reproduced.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="essay-body">
    <p>Suppose two groups hear the same recording. Both recover the words, but one reports easy understanding while the other reports effort and a strong accent. This imagined comparison separates three possible findings without deciding in advance why they differ. A stable difference between the groups might concern familiarity, linguistic expectations or the task; it need not be random error, and it cannot simply be located inside the speaker.</p>

    <p>The difficulty for a learner profile is that a judgement produced in an encounter can become a property attributed to one participant. A listener’s effort is real as an experience, but that does not establish that a speech defect caused it. At the same time, a judgement shaped by prejudice may predict a social penalty the speaker has good reason to avoid. The profile has to keep the measurement question and the practical question in relation without letting either settle the other.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="accent-construct-heading">
      <h2 id="accent-construct-heading">What each measure establishes</h2>

    <p>Munro and Derwing’s 1995 study distinguished three outcomes that an accent profile can too easily merge. Accentedness was rated difference from a target variety; comprehensibility was rated ease or difficulty of understanding; intelligibility was operationalised through the words listeners transcribed correctly. These definitions belong to the experimental tasks, and later studies may operationalise the outcomes differently. The distinction is useful precisely because the operation has to remain attached to the claim.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-1" href="#accent-note-1" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 1">1</a></sup></p>

    <p>The measures are related without being substitutes. Speech can be strongly accented and still permit high word recovery; a listener can obtain the words while reporting effort that a transcription score does not contain. A departure from the comparison variety therefore cannot be promoted into a failure of communication without another observation. Conversely, successful word transcription does not establish that an interaction required little effort or allowed the participants to repair misunderstandings.</p>

    <p>Saito’s 2021 meta-analyses show why this matters to intervention. Across 37 listener studies, comprehensibility related to segmental, prosodic and temporal features, whereas accentedness was especially associated with segmental accuracy. Across 17 instructional studies, gains were larger for comprehensibility than for nativelikeness. The feature that makes a voice most noticeably different is consequently not guaranteed to be the feature whose alteration would most improve understanding. A target described only as reducing accent conceals which outcome the work is meant to change.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-2" href="#accent-note-2" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 2">2</a></sup></p>

    <p>A phonetic transcription supplies a different kind of evidence again. It records an analyst’s account of a token against a declared comparison system. It can identify a realised contrast or a departure from the expected form; it cannot by itself show that a listener missed the word, expended more effort or assigned a social identity. A profile that moves directly from a transcription to a learner’s need has skipped the observation through which the proposed consequence would have to be established.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="accent-bias-heading">
      <h2 id="accent-bias-heading">Agreement does not locate the cause</h2>

    <p>Rater agreement is necessary to know whether listeners produce a consistent result. It is not sufficient to know what the result measures. Lindemann and Subtirelu’s account of listeners who are “reliably biased” makes the distinction explicit: shared expectations about a speaker’s linguistic or social group can enter stable judgements. Increasing agreement under those conditions can make an instrument more precise about a listening norm without making the norm acoustically or socially neutral.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-3" href="#accent-note-3" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 3">3</a></sup></p>

    <p>This is a possibility to test, not a reason to presume that every listener group must disagree. Isaacs and Thomson compared 20 experienced and 20 novice raters, using five- or nine-point scales to judge 38 speakers. Their ratings showed high internal consistency and no mean group differences by experience or scale length, although rating strategies and use of the longer scale’s middle differed. The null differences constrain the relational account: listener participation in the measure does not entail an observable difference between every pair of listener populations.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-4" href="#accent-note-4" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 4">4</a></sup></p>

    <p>A similar mean can arise in different populations and still answer different practical questions. An unfamiliar client’s first encounter, a colleague’s routine exchange and an examiner’s judgement are not interchangeable merely because their average ratings converge. The profile must specify whose response it is intended to predict, then examine whether the task and sample make that prediction credible.</p>

    <p>Where the intended population is discriminatory, predictive success produces a further problem. A rating may forecast a real penalty without identifying a legitimate pronunciation target. Discarding the prediction because the penalty is unjust can leave the speaker unprepared; treating the prediction as a diagnosis converts the gatekeeper’s practice into the speaker’s deficit. Neither error is repaired by a reliability coefficient, because consistency concerns the judgement’s recurrence rather than the distribution of responsibility for it.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="accent-accommodation-heading">
      <h2 id="accent-accommodation-heading">Who is being asked to adapt?</h2>

    <p>Gass and Varonis’s study identifies familiarity as one contributor to comprehensibility, including familiarity with the topic, non-native speech, an accent and an individual speaker. Its reported prominence of topic familiarity is a reminder that the communicative conditions extend beyond pronunciation. The result supports examining those conditions; it should not be made a universal estimate of how much exposure will improve every encounter.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-5" href="#accent-note-5" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 5">5</a></sup></p>

    <p>In a workplace profile, first contact and continuing cooperation would therefore need different treatment. The former asks how a listener without prior familiarity understands a sample; the latter asks what regular participants can learn and how they repair difficulty together. Averaging the two into a single property of the speaker would erase a potential route to improvement through the listeners and the conditions of interaction.</p>

    <p>Speaker-side work remains a legitimate route. Pacing, emphasis and repair may serve a comprehensibility goal, while a person may choose a particular accent for performance, identity or anticipated social treatment. Levis’s nativeness and intelligibility principles distinguish the aims without determining which must govern every case (Levis, 2005). A desired resemblance to a variety and a need to be understood can justify different exercises; the profile should make the chosen aim explicit rather than allowing one to borrow the other’s authority.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-6" href="#accent-note-6" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 6">6</a></sup></p>

    <p>ASHA treats accent modification as elective, distinguishes accent from communication disorder, and itself recognises the cultural pressures under which a service may be sought. The pressure is therefore not an objection its account simply forgot. It is the continuing difficulty of offering a chosen service in conditions where refusing it can be costly. A request can be informed and considered while still responding to discrimination.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="accent-ref-7" href="#accent-note-7" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 7">7</a></sup></p>

    <p>The analyst should not resolve that difficulty by overruling the request on the speaker’s behalf. Nor should accepting it imply that the listener’s demand has become a linguistic necessity. The same exercise can serve an actor’s chosen role, a learner’s preference or a worker’s attempt to evade a penalty; those purposes change what success means. Consent to the exercise cannot establish that the burden ought to have fallen on that person in the first place.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="accent-task-heading">
      <h2 id="accent-task-heading">From a sample to a bounded recommendation</h2>

    <p>The elicitation task sets another limit on attribution. A reading passage fixes vocabulary and syntax and permits monitoring. A word list isolates contrasts while removing contextual prediction. Spontaneous speech introduces planning, hesitation and repair; conversation adds a participant who can infer and accommodate. These tasks do not simply yield cleaner and noisier versions of the same underlying performance. They ask the speaker to do different things.</p>

    <p>A careful transcript can consequently establish how those items were produced on that occasion without establishing how the person speaks across settings. Improvement on a practised list may reflect familiarity with the items rather than transfer. If the concern is a telephone exchange, a face-to-face task supplies visual information the intended setting withholds. If the concern is interactional repair, isolated word recovery observes only one part of the event.</p>

    <p>The recommendation has to connect an observed difficulty to a declared goal under relevant conditions. Separate evidence of word recovery, reported effort and perceived accent can locate which outcome changed; a new but related task can test transfer; more than one listener or transcriber can expose disagreement. The purpose of collecting these accounts is to find the limit of the inference, not merely to accumulate support for a judgement already made from a transcript.</p>

    <p>That leaves some findings irreducibly in tension. A feature can leave word recovery intact, increase perceived accent and predict an adverse social response. The speaker may choose work directed at the third outcome even where the first supplies no reason for it. A profile can support that choice while recording that its aim is to navigate a particular demand, and while identifying what listener exposure, task redesign or opportunities for repair could change elsewhere in the encounter. It then has to evaluate the intervention against the goal actually chosen, rather than announce that the speaker’s English has improved in general.</p>
    </section>

